---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const heroImage = "/images/home/cosmic-oceans-hero.png";

const journeyItems = [
  {
    title: "The Fourteen Lokas",
    href: "/14-lokas",
    eyebrow: "Layered worlds",
    description: "The layered worlds of existence: seven higher Lokas, Bhuloka, and seven lower Lokas.",
    status: "available",
  },
  {
    title: "Mount Meru",
    href: "/mount-meru",
    eyebrow: "Cosmic axis",
    description: "The cosmic axis at the center of the universe, where Meru is introduced as the pillar of cosmic order.",
    status: "available",
  },
  {
    title: "The Sapta Dvipa",
    href: "/sapta-dvipa",
    eyebrow: "Seven continents",
    description: "The seven great continents of Puranic cosmic geography.",
    status: "available",
  },
  {
    title: "The Cosmic Oceans",
    href: "/cosmic-oceans",
    eyebrow: "Cosmic boundaries",
    description: "The seven oceans that separate the great Dvipas: salt, sugarcane juice, sura, ghee, curd, milk, and pure sweet water.",
    status: "available",
  },
  {
    title: "The Higher Worlds",
    href: "/14-lokas",
    eyebrow: "Upper realms",
    description: "Where sages, celestial beings, devas, and higher states of existence are described.",
    status: "available",
  },
  {
    title: "The Lower Worlds",
    href: "/14-lokas",
    eyebrow: "Hidden realms",
    description: "Mysterious realms beneath the Earth, associated with powerful beings, hidden cities, and treasures.",
    status: "available",
  },
];

const related = [
  { title: "The Sapta Dvipa", href: "/sapta-dvipa", image: "/images/home/sapta-dvipa-hero.png", eyebrow: "Seven continents" },
  { title: "Mount Meru", href: "/mount-meru", image: "/images/home/mount-meru-hero.png", eyebrow: "Cosmic axis" },
  { title: "The Hindu Cosmic Universe", href: "/cosmic-universe", image: "/images/home/home-hero.webp", eyebrow: "Cosmic structure" },
];

const concept = {
  title: "The Seven Cosmic Oceans - Seas Beyond Imagination",
  eyebrow: "Chapter 4",
  summary: "The Puranas describe seven immense cosmic oceans arranged around the Sapta Dvipa: seas of salt water, sugarcane juice, sura, ghee, curd, milk, and pure sweet water.",
  image: heroImage,
  transitionName: "concept-cosmic-oceans",
  posterHero: true,
  readingLayout: "article",
  accent: "#65d7dc",
  facts: ["Lavana Samudra", "Kshira Samudra", "Seven boundaries", "Samudra Manthana"],
  journeyItems,
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Oceans That Mark the Boundaries of Worlds",
      paragraphs: [
        "At first, the pattern seems simple.",
        "A continent.",
        "An ocean.",
        "Another continent.",
        "Another ocean.",
        "But as the journey continues, something remarkable becomes clear.",
        "Nothing in this universe is placed at random.",
        "The farther you travel from Mount Meru, the more the cosmic design reveals itself.",
        "The Puranas describe the Seven Continents and the Seven Cosmic Oceans as perfectly arranged concentric rings, expanding outward from the center of creation.",
        "Each continent is embraced by its own ocean.",
        "Beyond that ocean lies another continent.",
        "Beyond that, another ocean.",
        "The pattern continues again and again until the universe reaches the distant shores of Pushkara Dvipa, the outermost of the Seven Continents.",
        "Even their proportions follow a divine order.",
        "Each ocean is said to be equal in width to the continent it surrounds.",
        "The result is a universe built with remarkable balance and symmetry - a design where every boundary has purpose and every realm has its appointed place.",
        "These oceans are not empty spaces separating distant lands.",
        "They are thresholds.",
        "To cross one is to leave behind an entire world and enter another.",
        "New mountains rise on the horizon.",
        "Different rivers flow across the land.",
        "New kingdoms flourish.",
        "Different beings inhabit these realms.",
        "Even the forms of worship described in the Puranas vary from one continent to the next.",
        "With every ocean crossed, the universe becomes larger, richer, and more wondrous than before.",
        "The journey is no longer across geography alone.",
        "It is a journey through the sacred architecture of the cosmos itself.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "1. Lavana Samudra - The Ocean of Salt Water",
      paragraphs: [
        "The first ocean surrounding Jambudvipa is Lavana Samudra, the Ocean of Salt Water.",
        "Of all the cosmic oceans, this is the most familiar.",
        "Its salty waters resemble the seas of our own world, making it the easiest for readers to imagine.",
        "Beyond its vast expanse lies Plaksha Dvipa, the second of the Seven Continents.",
        "Here, the journey begins to move beyond the ordinary world and into increasingly extraordinary realms.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "2. Ikshu-rasa Samudra - The Ocean of Sugarcane Juice",
      paragraphs: [
        "Encircling Plaksha Dvipa is Ikshu-rasa Samudra, the Ocean of Sugarcane Juice.",
        "The Sanskrit word Ikshu means sugarcane, while rasa means juice or essence.",
        "In ancient Indian tradition, sugarcane represents sweetness, nourishment, prosperity, and the abundance of nature.",
        "Its presence in the cosmic universe reminds the reader that the Puranas describe a creation overflowing with richness far beyond ordinary human experience.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "3. Sura Samudra - The Ocean of Sura",
      paragraphs: [
        "Beyond Shalmala Dvipa lies Sura Samudra.",
        "The Sanskrit word Sura refers to a sacred fermented beverage mentioned in Vedic and Puranic literature.",
        "Although it is sometimes translated simply as \"wine,\" the original Sanskrit term carries a broader cultural and ritual significance than the modern word suggests.",
        "The Puranas present this ocean as another marvelous feature of the cosmic landscape, illustrating that each successive realm possesses its own unique character.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "4. Ghrita Samudra - The Ocean of Clarified Butter",
      paragraphs: [
        "Surrounding Kusha Dvipa is Ghrita Samudra, the Ocean of Clarified Butter, commonly known as ghee.",
        "Few substances hold greater importance in Vedic tradition.",
        "Ghee is offered into the sacred fire during yajnas, used in countless religious ceremonies, and regarded as a symbol of purity, nourishment, and divine blessing.",
        "Its appearance as one of the cosmic oceans reflects the sacred nature of the universe itself.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "5. Dadhi Samudra - The Ocean of Curd",
      paragraphs: [
        "Beyond Krauncha Dvipa lies Dadhi Samudra, the Ocean of Curd.",
        "Curd has occupied an important place in Indian life for thousands of years.",
        "It is associated with prosperity, well-being, fertility, and auspiciousness.",
        "From temple offerings to traditional rituals, it remains one of the most revered foods in Hindu culture.",
        "Within the cosmic geography, Dadhi Samudra continues the sequence of sacred substances separating one continent from another.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "6. Kshira Samudra - The Ocean of Milk",
      paragraphs: [
        "Among all the cosmic oceans, none is more famous than Kshira Samudra, the Ocean of Milk.",
        "It surrounds Shaka Dvipa and occupies a special place throughout Hindu sacred literature.",
        "According to the Puranas, it was here that the Devas and Asuras joined together in the great Samudra Manthana, the Churning of the Ocean of Milk.",
        "Using Mount Mandara as the churning rod and the great serpent Vasuki as the rope, they churned the ocean in search of Amrita, the nectar of immortality.",
        "From its depths emerged many of Hindu tradition's most celebrated treasures - the goddess Lakshmi, Kamadhenu, Airavata, Uchchaihshravas, the celestial jewel Kaustubha, the divine physician Dhanvantari carrying the pot of Amrita, and many other sacred beings and objects.",
        "Because of this event, Kshira Samudra is remembered not only as part of the cosmic geography but also as the setting for one of the greatest episodes in the Puranas.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "7. Svadu-jala Samudra - The Ocean of Pure, Sweet Water",
      paragraphs: [
        "Beyond Pushkara Dvipa, the outermost of the Seven Continents, lies Svadu-jala Samudra.",
        "The Sanskrit words Svadu and Jala mean \"sweet\" and \"water.\"",
        "This final ocean forms the outer boundary of the inhabited world described in the Puranas.",
        "Beyond it, the scriptures speak of regions that lead toward the limits of the material universe itself.",
        "The journey has reached the farthest edge of the cosmic world.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Literal or Symbolic?",
      paragraphs: [
        "For centuries, scholars, philosophers, and spiritual teachers have reflected upon the meaning of these remarkable oceans.",
        "Some traditions understand them as literal features of the universe described in the Puranas.",
        "Others see them as symbolic representations of abundance, purity, sacrifice, nourishment, or different stages of spiritual understanding.",
        "The cosmological chapters of the Puranas themselves primarily present these oceans as integral parts of the universe's structure, without insisting on a single philosophical interpretation.",
        "This openness has allowed readers across generations to appreciate these descriptions from historical, theological, symbolic, and devotional perspectives.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "A Universe of Order and Wonder",
      paragraphs: [
        "What makes the Seven Cosmic Oceans remarkable is not merely the substances they contain.",
        "It is the vision they create.",
        "Every continent is carefully separated.",
        "Every boundary has meaning.",
        "Every ocean mirrors the continent beside it.",
        "Every ring expands the universe in perfect harmony.",
        "Rather than depicting an empty expanse of space, the Puranas present a cosmos filled with balance, beauty, diversity, and purpose.",
        "The oceans are not empty distances between lands.",
        "They are part of the very architecture of creation.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Beyond the Cosmic Oceans",
      paragraphs: [
        "Our journey has now carried us outward from Mount Meru, across the Seven Continents, and through the Seven Cosmic Oceans.",
        "Yet we have explored only the broad outline of this extraordinary universe.",
        "Among all the continents described in the Puranas, one receives far greater attention than the others.",
        "It is the continent at the very center.",
        "The land surrounding Mount Meru.",
        "The only region where human beings are said to perform karma, pursue Dharma, and strive for Moksha.",
        "It is called Jambudvipa.",
        "In the next chapter, we leave the broad map behind and step into the heart of the Puranic universe, exploring the sacred continent where the story of humanity unfolds.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Scriptural References",
      paragraphs: ["Primary Sources"],
      list: [
        "Bhagavata Purana, Canto 5, Chapters 20-21.",
        "Vishnu Purana, Book II, Chapters 4-7.",
        "Brahmanda Purana, Cosmographical Sections.",
        "Matsya Purana, Cosmological Sections.",
        "Vayu Purana, Cosmographical Sections.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Editorial Note",
      paragraphs: [
        "The major Puranas consistently describe seven concentric oceans surrounding the Sapta Dvipa. Throughout this book, the original Sanskrit names - such as Lavana Samudra, Kshira Samudra, and Ghrita Samudra - are used alongside their English meanings to remain faithful to the traditional texts while ensuring clarity for modern readers.",
      ],
    },
  ],
  related,
};
const referenceItems = concept.sections.find((section) => section.title === "Scriptural References")?.list ?? [];
const editorialNote = concept.sections.find((section) => section.title === "Editorial Note");
const readingSections = concept.sections.filter(
  (section) => section.title !== "Scriptural References" && section.title !== "Editorial Note",
);
const oceanRings = [
  { name: "Lavana Samudra", meaning: "Salt Water" },
  { name: "Ikshu-rasa Samudra", meaning: "Sugarcane Juice" },
  { name: "Sura Samudra", meaning: "Sura" },
  { name: "Ghrita Samudra", meaning: "Ghee" },
  { name: "Dadhi Samudra", meaning: "Curd" },
  { name: "Kshira Samudra", meaning: "Milk" },
  { name: "Svadu-jala Samudra", meaning: "Pure Sweet Water" },
];
const nextChapter = {
  eyebrow: "Continue next",
  title: "Jambudvipa",
  description: "After the full map of continents and oceans, the journey returns to the central land where human life unfolds in the Puranic worldview.",
  href: "/library",
  cta: "Open the Library ->",
  image: "/images/home/home-hero.webp",
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${concept.title} | Sanatana Path`} description={concept.summary} image={heroImage} type="article">
  <div data-reading-body class="chapter-page oceans-page">
    <section class="chapter-hero">
      <div class="hero-copy glass-panel">
        <a class="home-pill" href="/cosmic-universe">Home</a>
        <p class="eyebrow">{concept.eyebrow}</p>
        <h1>{concept.title}</h1>
        <p class="summary">{concept.summary}</p>
        <div class="fact-row">
          {concept.facts.map((fact) => <span>{fact}</span>)}
        </div>
      </div>

    </section>

    <nav class="journey-rail" aria-label="Cosmic Universe chapters">
      <div>
        <span>Journey 2</span>
        <strong>Cosmic Universe</strong>
      </div>
      <ol>
        {journeyItems.map((item, index) => (
          <li class={item.href === "/cosmic-oceans" ? "active" : ""}>
            <a href={item.href}>
              <small>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</small>
              <span>{item.title}</span>
              <em>{item.eyebrow}</em>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section class="visual-guide" id="structure">
      <div class="guide-copy glass-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">See the boundary</p>
        <h2>Every ocean is a threshold between worlds.</h2>
        <p>
          This chapter is easier to understand when the oceans are seen as ordered rings: each sea surrounds a continent and opens the way to another realm.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="guide-image">
        <img src={heroImage} alt="Concentric cosmic oceans as sacred boundaries" />
        <figcaption>Seven oceans surrounding the cosmic continents</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="ring-stack" aria-label="The seven cosmic oceans">
        {oceanRings.map((ring, index) => (
          <div class="ring-chip ocean-chip">
            <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
            <strong>{ring.name}</strong>
            <em>{ring.meaning}</em>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-layout">
      <article class="reading-panel">
        {readingSections.map((section, index) => (
          <section class="chapter-section" id={`section-${index + 1}`}>
            <span class="section-number">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
            <h2>{section.title}</h2>
            {section.paragraphs?.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
            {section.list && <ul>{section.list.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>}
          </section>
        ))}

        <section class="reference-drawer" id="references">
          <details>
            <summary>
              <span>Optional source notes</span>
              <strong>Scriptural References</strong>
            </summary>
            <ul>
              {referenceItems.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </details>
        </section>

        {editorialNote && (
          <section class="editorial-note">
            <p class="eyebrow">Editorial note</p>
            {editorialNote.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
          </section>
        )}
      </article>

      <aside class="chapter-side">
        <div class="side-card jump-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Jump inside</p>
          <div>
            <a href="#structure">Structure</a>
            <a href="#section-2">Ocean list</a>
            <a href="#section-9">Meaning</a>
            <a href="#references">References</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="side-card related-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Related screen</p>
          {related.map((item) => (
            <a class="related-link" href={item.href} style={`--related-image: url('${item.image}')`}>
              <span>{item.eyebrow}</span>
              <strong>{item.title}</strong>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="next-chapter" style={`--next-image: url('${nextChapter.image}')`}>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">{nextChapter.eyebrow}</p>
        <h2>{nextChapter.title}</h2>
        <p>{nextChapter.description}</p>
      </div>
      <a href={nextChapter.href}>{nextChapter.cta}</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .chapter-page {
    --ink: light-dark(rgb(43 33 24 / 1), #fff5df);
    --muted: light-dark(rgb(43 33 24 / 0.74), rgba(255, 245, 223, 0.74));
    --line: light-dark(rgb(54 102 103 / 0.22), rgba(126, 238, 239, 0.22));
    --gold: light-dark(rgb(103 83 40 / 1), #f0c15d);
    --aqua: light-dark(rgb(55 105 104 / 1), #7ff4f2);
    max-width: 1840px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(18px, 3vw, 46px);
    color: var(--ink);
    background:
      linear-gradient(rgba(126, 238, 239, 0.035) 1px, transparent 1px),
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(126, 238, 239, 0.035) 1px, transparent 1px);
    background-size: 128px 128px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    color: var(--aqua);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .chapter-hero,
  .visual-guide,
  .next-chapter,
  .side-card,
  .chapter-section,
  .reference-drawer details,
  .editorial-note,
  .journey-rail {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.76), rgba(2, 17, 21, 0.76));
    box-shadow: 0 28px 90px light-dark(rgb(91 69 38 / 0.077), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.22));
  }

  .chapter-hero {
    display: block;
    max-width: 1040px;
    min-height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: 24px;
  }

  .hero-copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    margin: 0;
    padding: clamp(22px, 3.2vw, 36px);
    border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.28), rgba(240, 193, 93, 0.28));
    border-radius: 24px;
    background:
      linear-gradient(135deg, light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.94), rgba(2, 10, 14, 0.94)), light-dark(rgb(250 245 234 / 0.8), rgba(5, 32, 36, 0.8))),
      radial-gradient(circle at 85% 18%, rgba(240, 193, 93, 0.16), transparent 34%);
    backdrop-filter: blur(18px);
  }

  .home-pill {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
    padding: 12px 18px;
    border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.5), rgba(255, 141, 98, 0.5));
    border-radius: 34px;
    color: light-dark(rgb(110 67 50 / 1), #ff9b74);
    font-weight: 900;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .hero-copy h1,
  .guide-copy h2,
  .chapter-section h2,
  .next-chapter h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .hero-copy h1 {
    max-width: 900px;
    font-size: clamp(2.45rem, 5vw, 5.2rem);
    line-height: 0.96;
  }

  .summary,
  .guide-copy p,
  .chapter-section p,
  .next-chapter p,
  .editorial-note p {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.15vw, 1.15rem);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .fact-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-top: 26px;
  }

  .fact-row span,
  .ring-chip,
  .chapter-side a,
  .next-chapter a {
    border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.38), rgba(126, 238, 239, 0.38));
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: light-dark(rgb(250 245 234 / 0.72), rgba(4, 31, 36, 0.72));
  }

  .fact-row span {
    padding: 10px 14px;
    color: var(--aqua);
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .guide-image img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .journey-rail {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 180px 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: center;
    margin: clamp(16px, 2.4vw, 28px) 0;
    padding: 12px 18px;
    border-radius: 34px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .journey-rail > div > span,
  .journey-rail small,
  .journey-rail em {
    color: var(--gold);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    line-height: 1.15;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .journey-rail strong {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .journey-rail ol {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .journey-rail a {
    display: grid;
    gap: 3px;
    min-width: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .journey-rail a > span {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(0.84rem, 1vw, 1.02rem);
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    text-transform: none;
  }

  .journey-rail a > em {
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }

  .journey-rail li.active a span,
  .journey-rail li.active a em {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .visual-guide {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 0.82fr 1.25fr 0.82fr;
    gap: clamp(16px, 2vw, 28px);
    align-items: stretch;
    padding: clamp(18px, 2.8vw, 36px);
    border-radius: 28px;
  }

  .guide-copy,
  .guide-image,
  .ring-stack {
    border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.18), rgba(126, 238, 239, 0.18));
    border-radius: 22px;
    background: light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.54), rgba(0, 12, 16, 0.54));
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .guide-copy {
    padding: clamp(18px, 2.4vw, 28px);
  }

  .guide-copy h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.85rem, 3vw, 3.35rem);
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .guide-image {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 300px;
  }

  .guide-image figcaption {
    position: absolute;
    left: 18px;
    bottom: 18px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.68), rgba(0, 10, 12, 0.68));
    color: var(--aqua);
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .ring-stack {
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    padding: 16px;
    align-content: center;
  }

  .ring-chip {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 38px 1fr;
    gap: 5px 12px;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 13px 14px;
    border-radius: 16px;
  }

  .ring-chip span,
  .section-number {
    display: inline-grid;
    width: 38px;
    height: 38px;
    place-items: center;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: rgba(240, 193, 93, 0.16);
    color: var(--gold);
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .ring-chip em {
    grid-column: 2;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .content-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(260px, 330px);
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px);
    margin-top: clamp(22px, 3vw, 42px);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .reading-panel {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
  }

  .chapter-section,
  .reference-drawer details,
  .editorial-note {
    border-radius: 24px;
    padding: clamp(22px, 2.8vw, 38px);
  }

  .chapter-section h2 {
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: clamp(1.85rem, 3vw, 3.55rem);
    line-height: 1.04;
  }

  .chapter-section ul,
  .reference-drawer ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    padding-left: 22px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .chapter-side {
    position: sticky;
    top: 88px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .side-card {
    border-radius: 20px;
    padding: 16px;
  }

  .jump-card div {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
